 ## Overview
-Truncated Depth-Based Sampling (TDBS) is a ray sampling strategy that utilizes monocular depth priors informed by a truncated normal distribution to optimize ray point sampling, improving convergence speed and accuracy of pose estimation in joint NeRF and camera pose optimization [S1]. It sets itself apart from alternatives like NoPe-NeRF by explicitly modeling the probability of surface location around the depth prior using a truncated normal distribution, rather than merely considering overall depth distortion [S1].
+Truncated Depth-Based Sampling (TDBS) is a ray sampling strategy for Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) that utilizes monocular depth priors to concentrate samples near estimated surface locations, thereby improving convergence speed and pose estimation accuracy in joint optimization scenarios [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that may use depth for supervision or coarse-to-fine alternation, TDBS relies exclusively on truncated normal distributions centered on depth priors for sampling throughout the training process [S1, Subtopics].
 
